WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.367
GPU cost controls need ongoing operation.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:06.524
A useful dashboard connects spend to workload purpose, owner, environment, utilization,

00:00:06.524 --> 00:00:10.681
job success rate, failure rate, idle time, queue time, and trend.

00:00:10.681 --> 00:00:12.571
Spend alone says what happened.

00:00:12.571 --> 00:00:14.838
Spend with context helps explain why.

00:00:14.838 --> 00:00:17.484
Anomaly alerts help catch unexpected usage early.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:22.019
A forgotten notebook, runaway retry loop, sudden inference traffic change, failed cleanup,

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:27.687
or unusual data transfer pattern should be visible before it becomes a major budget problem.

00:00:27.687 --> 00:00:30.711
Alerts should route to owners who can act.

00:00:30.711 --> 00:00:34.112
Forecasting is more useful when it includes planned work.

00:00:34.112 --> 00:00:36.757
Experiments, launches, evaluations, seasonal traffic, model refreshes,

00:00:36.757 --> 00:00:39.780
and migration projects can all change GPU demand.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.182
Forecasts should combine historical spend with known upcoming events.

00:00:43.182 --> 00:00:46.583
Showback or chargeback can improve accountability when used constructively.

00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:49.228
The goal is not to shame teams.

00:00:49.228 --> 00:00:53.763
The goal is to help teams understand the cost of their choices

00:00:53.763 --> 00:00:56.030
and compare spend against business value.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.676
Regular cost reviews turn data into decisions.

00:00:58.676 --> 00:01:02.833
A review may lead to resizing, rescheduling, cleanup, caching, model optimization,

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:05.100
policy changes, or retiring a workload.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.879
The best reviews produce concrete engineering actions, not just reports.

00:01:08.879 --> 00:01:13.792
Reviews are most effective when they include both finance context and engineering context.

00:01:13.792 --> 00:01:16.437
The cost chart shows where money went.

00:01:16.437 --> 00:01:21.728
The engineering discussion explains whether that spend created value and what should change next.

00:01:21.728 --> 00:01:26.641
A recurring review also helps teams notice patterns, such as repeated failed jobs,

00:01:26.641 --> 00:01:30.420
unused datasets, or inference capacity that no longer matches demand.
